<section class="nc-guide hub-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Which calculator to use</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Deciding where to list something you already own:</strong> the <a href="../../calculators/marketplace.html">marketplace payout calculator</a> compares what you keep on each platform at the same price, or the price each platform needs for the same payout.</li>
      <li><strong>Selling event tickets:</strong> the <a href="../../calculators/ticket-resale.html">ticket resale calculator</a> adds the buyer's side, since resale buyers compare all-in prices.</li>
      <li><strong>Deciding what to pay for inventory:</strong> the <a href="../../calculators/max-buy-price.html">max buy price calculator</a> works backward from the expected sale price to the most you can pay and still hit your profit or return target.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Work backward from the payout, not forward from the price</h2>
    <p>Most platforms take a percentage of the sale plus a fixed per-order fee. That makes the price you need to list at higher than "what I want plus the fee rate." To net $50 on a platform charging 13% plus $0.40, the list price is ($50 + $0.40) ÷ (1 − 0.13) = $57.93, not $56.50. Both calculators do this division for you, and it matters more as the fixed fee grows relative to the price: on cheap items, the per-order fee can take a bigger share than the percentage.</p>

    <h2>Buy right, and the sale takes care of itself</h2>
    <p>Profit is mostly decided when you buy. Say an item sells for $100, fees are 15%, and shipping costs you $9: you net $76. To earn a 30% return on what you paid, the most you can pay is $76 ÷ 1.30 = $58.46. Pay $70 and the same sale returns under 9%. Run the numbers before you buy, using a realistic sold price from completed listings rather than asking prices.</p>

    <h2>Costs sellers forget</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Shipping supplies</strong>, including boxes, mailers, tape, and labels, which add up across hundreds of orders.</li>
      <li><strong>Returns.</strong> On platforms that allow them, a share of sales come back with a shipping cost and sometimes damage.</li>
      <li><strong>Payment and promotion fees.</strong> Promoted listings, payment processing on some platforms, and currency conversion are separate from the headline fee.</li>
      <li><strong>Taxes.</strong> Marketplaces may report your sales to the IRS on Form 1099-K. Profit on items sold for more than you paid is generally taxable; keep purchase records.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Fee schedules change several times a year and vary by category and seller level. Every rate in these calculators can be overridden; confirm against the fee preview in your listing before relying on a number.</p>
  </div>
</section>
